<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sima Xin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/203-bce">203 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sima Xin was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> officer who surrendered with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-han">Zhang Han</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> in 207 BCE. In 206 BCE Xiang Yu made him King of Sai, one of the three kingdoms carved from the Qin homeland. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> conquered Sai the same year; Sima Xin later rejoined Xiang Yu and killed himself after a defeat at Chenggao in 203 BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sima Xin began his career as a prison officer in Yueyang County, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>. When Xiang Liang was arrested after trouble with the law, he asked Cao Jiu, a prison officer in Qi County, to write to Sima Xin on his behalf, and with Sima Xin's help Xiang Liang was pardoned and released.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 209 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dazexiang-uprising">Dazexiang uprising</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-sheng">Chen Sheng</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-guang">Wu Guang</a> set off rebellions across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. When Chen Sheng's deputy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-wen">Zhou Wen</a> came close to the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a>, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-er-shi">Qin Er Shi</a> accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-han">Zhang Han</a>'s plan to pardon the convict labourers at the Mausoleum of the First Qin Emperor and enlist them, and sent Sima Xin, then Chief Clerk, and the Commandant Dong Yi as Zhang Han's deputies. Their army destroyed Chen Sheng's group, defeated the Wei and Qi rebels and killed Xiang Liang at the Battle of Dingtao.</p><p>In 207 BCE Xiang Yu defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-han">Zhang Han</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-julu">Battle of Julu</a>. Zhang Han sent Sima Xin to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a> for reinforcements, but the emperor, deceived by Zhao Gao, refused. Sima Xin escaped Zhao Gao's assassins on the way back and reported that the government was in Zhao Gao's hands, and Zhang Han, his deputies and 200,000 troops then surrendered to Xiang Yu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> divided the former empire into the Eighteen Kingdoms after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> in 206 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-han">Zhang Han</a>, Sima Xin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-yi">Dong Yi</a> received the three kingdoms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a> region, the Qin heartland, known together as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-qins">Three Qins</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>, the King of Han, took Zhang Han's kingdom in a surprise attack, Sima Xin and Dong Yi surrendered to him, but they went over to Xiang Yu after Liu Bang's defeat at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuzhou">Pengcheng</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chu-han-contention">Chu–Han Contention</a>.</p><p>At the Battle of Chenggao Sima Xin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-yi">Dong Yi</a> fought under Cao Jiu against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>, who lured their forces into an ambush near the Si River. All three killed themselves after the defeat. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-han">Book of Han</a> records that Liu Bang had Sima Xin's head cut off and hung on a pole in Yueyang County as punishment for his betrayal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sima Xin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Like <b>Sima Xin</b> of the Kingdom of Sai, he later returned to Xiang Yu and killed himself after the Chu defeat on the Si River near Chenggao in 203 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-di-206-bc">Kingdom of Di (206 BC)</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sima Xin</b> had been chief clerk under the Qin general Zhang Han and surrendered with him to Xiang Yu in 207 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-sai">Kingdom of Sai</a></li></ul></section>
